ANIX(1)                  A-NIX 0.1T Manual                  ANIX(1)

NAME
    anix - a small *NIX-style command shell for the
    Raspberry Pi Pico, Pico W, Pico 2 and Pico 2 W, running under
    MicroPython. Version 0.1T.

SYNOPSIS
    The board starts the shell at every power-on and reset. Programs
    that should start by themselves go in /etc/crontab (@boot lines).
    Hold BOOTSEL while it starts for safe mode: the shell without
    /etc/profile, /etc/startup, /etc/crontab and the watchdog.

DESCRIPTION
    A-NIX is an interactive shell with a small *NIX-flavored
    layout: a $PATH of standalone programs under /bin (one file
    per command, exec'd on demand, exactly the way a real *NIX
    shell resolves and runs a command it found on PATH), a small
    set of true shell builtins that need the shell's own process
    state (cd, export, ...), environment variables, and shell
    scripts with if/elif/else, for and while loops (nestable).

    It is not a full POSIX shell - one interpreter (shell.py, in
    /lib) reads a line, splits it into words ("quotes" group words),
    and either runs a builtin directly or execs the matching /bin
    file. Output can go to a file (> >>) or into the next command
    (|). No subshells; one background job (cmd &), no fg/bg.

STARTUP SEQUENCE
    /boot.py and /main.py run on every power-up/reset (must stay at
    the filesystem root - the firmware only auto-runs files found
    there).

    boot.py drives low every output pin left high (after a power-on
    they already are; after a soft reset - Ctrl-D, Thonny, mpremote -
    outputs keep their state), and sets up BOOTSEL and the status LED
    for main.py. It runs on every start, also after a soft reset.

    main.py blinks the LED once, logs the boot, checks BOOTSEL (held
    -> safe mode) and starts the shell. It is skipped after a soft
    reset from a raw-REPL tool (Thonny, mpremote).

    Inside the shell:
      1. ENV starts empty; /etc/anix.conf (settings), then
         /etc/profile (HOME, PATH, aliases) load; the shell watchdog
         starts if WATCHDOG=on.
      2. The shell changes into $HOME.
      3. /etc/startup runs as a startup script.
      4. cron reads /etc/crontab and runs its @boot lines, in order
         (once per board start, not again on `exit`).
      5. The interactive prompt starts.

    `exit` restarts the shell from step 1 (output pins stay as they
    are - a background job keeps its pins). Ctrl-C at the prompt
    leaves to the raw REPL (Thonny and mpremote rely on that to
    connect); Ctrl-D there soft-resets and runs boot.py/main.py again.

    Every boot is logged; BOOTSEL gives a safe mode - see SAFE MODE
    AND THE BOOT LOG, below.

FILESYSTEM
    /                 boot.py, main.py (must stay at the root)
    /lib/shell.py     the shell itself - the only file meant to be
                      imported rather than run by name
    /bin              every command but the builtins below - one
                      file per program, found via $PATH like a real
                      *NIX /bin
    /root             $HOME - where cd with no argument goes
    /etc/anix.conf    settings: WiFi, passwords, pins, logs, RAM,
                      watchdog - see `conf` and SETTINGS
    /etc/profile      shell environment (PATH, HOME, history) and
                      aliases, loaded after anix.conf
    /lib/script.py    the script language, loaded when a script runs
    /etc/startup      script run at every shell start
    /var/log          boot log and safe-mode state (see dmesg)
    /usr/share/man/anix        this page
    /usr/share/doc/copyright   license text (see `license`)

ENVIRONMENT
    Variables live only in the running shell's ENV dict - they do
    not persist across reboot on their own. Put anything you want
    every session in /etc/profile (export lines; read at every shell
    start). Board and service settings live in /etc/anix.conf - see
    SETTINGS; both files are read the same way, profile last.
    Commands to run at every start go in /etc/startup.
    Reference as $NAME or ${NAME}; an undefined name is left
    unexpanded rather than becoming empty.

    HOME    Home directory. Set by /etc/profile. Used by `cd` with
            no argument.
    PATH    Colon-separated search list for bare command names not
            found in cwd. Default /bin. Extend with, e.g.:
                export PATH=/bin:/root
            A name containing "/" is never PATH-searched.
    PS1     Prompt template. Unset = "$cwd $ " (the folder bold
            blue, see COLOURS). $VAR/${VAR} expand;
            the literal "\w" becomes the current directory:
                export PS1=\w>
    LINES, COLUMNS
            Screen size. Normally leave unset: the shell, `edit` and
            the dialogs ask the terminal for its real size. Set them
            only for a terminal that can't answer (`pg` uses LINES).
    HISTSIZE, HISTFILE
            How many prompt lines history keeps (default 50) and the
            file they're saved in (/etc/profile sets /var/history -
            outside /root, so clearing /root keeps it; none = not
            saved). Note: commands with passwords are saved too.
    TZ      Hours added to UTC by `date ntp`, e.g. 2 (Lithuanian
            winter time) or 3 (summer time).
    I2C_SDA, I2C_SCL, I2C_FREQ
            Default pins and clock for `i2c` (4, 5, 100000).
    WIFI_SSID, WIFI_PASS
            Read by `wifi` with no arguments, so you can just type
            `wifi` instead of `wifi myssid mypass` every time. Set
            with `conf` if this board always joins one network.
    WIFI_COUNTRY
            Two-letter country code for the WiFi radio, e.g. LT.
            Without it the radio uses worldwide settings and may
            not see networks on channels 12-13 (allowed in Europe).
    WEB_PINS
            Comma-separated list read by `httpd` with no pin
            arguments (see COMMANDS, httpd, below, for the
            ADCn/INn/PWMn/plain syntax it expects). none = no
            pins: the panel is only the file browser.
    HTTPD_FILES
            on (default) / off: the file browser in the httpd panel.
    HTTPD_PASS, TELNET_PASS
            Passwords for httpd and telnetd (plain text; a -p
            option on the command wins). none = -p is required.
    COLOR   on / off: colours on the screen - see COLOURS.

EXPANSIONS
    $?          exit status of the last command - "0" ok, "1" a
                command reported an error
    $((expr))   arithmetic: + - * / // % and parens; bare names
                (no $) pull from ENV, matching real sh arithmetic
    ${VAR}, $VAR   environment variable, braced or bare form
    $(command)     the command's output, newlines as spaces:
                   echo now: $(date)   log t.csv $(temp)
    $1..$9 $# $@ $0   arguments of the running script or function:
                   the 1st..9th, how many, all of them, its name
    "a b" 'a b'    quotes make one argument of several words; a
                   quoted argument is never glob-expanded (find .
                   "*.py") and never a redirection. Variables are
                   expanded inside quotes too.
    < file         the command reads file as its input, as from a
                   pipe: sort < names.txt   read LINE < cfg.txt
    2> file        send the command's errors to file (2>> appends)
    2>&1           errors go where the output goes (a file or pipe)
    > /dev/null    throw output away (2> /dev/null: errors)
    > file         send a command's output to file (replaces it).
                   Written safely: a temporary file replaces the old
                   one only when the command finishes - Ctrl-C or a
                   power cut leaves the old file. (So `cat a > a`
                   keeps a, unlike most shells.)
    >> file        append a command's output to file. Errors still
                   go to the screen. If the file doesn't end with a
                   line break, one is added first, so a new line
                   never sticks to the old last one. Works for
                   builtins and /bin commands alike: ls -l > list.txt
    a ; b          run a, then b
    a && b         run b only if a worked ($? = 0)
    a || b         run b only if a failed - e.g.
                   wifi && date ntp || echo no network
                   ; && || bind weaker than |, so each side may be a
                   pipe; a trailing & sends the whole line to the
                   background
    cmd &          run cmd in the background - see BACKGROUND JOB
    a | b          pipe: a's output becomes b's input. Commands
                   that read text read the pipe when given no file:
                   cat grep head tail wc sort uniq cut replace tee
                   hexdump sha256. Chains work:
                   cat log.txt | grep error | tail -n 5
                   Data passes in RAM up to 8 KB, beyond that through
                   a temporary file in /var/tmp. Errors go to the
                   screen, not into the pipe.

ALIASES
    alias name=command   `ll` then runs `ls -l`; arguments typed
                         after an alias are added: ll /bin
    alias                list them      unalias name   remove one
    Aliases last until the shell restarts; put `alias` lines in
    /etc/profile to keep them (two examples are there: ll and ..).
    An alias may use another alias.

BACKGROUND JOB
    cmd &           runs cmd on the second core; the prompt is free
                    at once. jobs shows it, kill stops it; when it
                    ends, the prompt says so with its $?.
    One job at a time, and not while tasks or another core-1 program
    run (see CORE 1). Its output is kept in RAM and written to
    /var/log/job.log by the shell on core 0, unless the command has
    its own > file - and new lines of it appear above
    the prompt by themselves, [1] in front, within about a second
    while you aren't typing (what you typed is kept). jobs -f
    follows the output live until the job ends or you press a key.
    Commands that need the keyboard or the screen (edit, watch,
    man ...) are refused. kill takes effect at the job's next output
    line or next command - a command busy without printing (a long
    wget) finishes that step first. The job's $? doesn't change yours.
    Example:
        wget http://server/big.bin &
        sh-loop.sh > readings.txt &

TASK SCHEDULER
    spawn file.py [args]   start a task         tasks   list them
    stop N / stop all      end tasks      tasks log   what they print
    Several of your own programs run together on core 1 while the
    shell stays free. A task file needs an async run:
        import asyncio, machine
        async def run(args):
            led = machine.Pin("LED", machine.Pin.OUT)
            while True:
                led.toggle()
                await asyncio.sleep(0.5)
    Each await lets the other tasks run - never use time.sleep() or a
    long loop without await in a task: it freezes them all. print()
    goes to /var/log/tasks.log ([N] prefix). An error ends only that
    task (traceback in the log); print() goes through RAM, the shell
    on core 0 writes the log (more than 4 KB while the shell is busy
    is dropped with a note). When the last task ends, core 1 is free
    again. Core 1 is shared: no `cmd &` while tasks run (see CORE 1).
    The prompt shows (N) while N tasks run. Start tasks at boot from
    /etc/startup, or @boot spawn x.py in /etc/crontab.

CORE 1
    Core 0 runs the shell, cron, telnetd and httpd. Core 1 runs ONE
    program at a time: the background job (cmd &), the task
    scheduler (spawn), or a program of your own. `ps` shows which.

    All of them start through /lib/core1.py. Core 1's stack must be
    one free block of RAM; when RAM is in pieces there may be none of
    16 KB although `free` shows plenty (its "largest free block" line
    tells). MicroPython 1.29 has a bug: a start that can't get its
    stack leaves core 1 blocked until the next reset. So A-NIX first
    checks that the block exists - 16 KB, else 12 (jobs and tasks need
    at least 12), else 8 for your own simple programs - and only then
    starts. If none exists: "no free block ... reboot" - start core-1
    programs early, e.g. as the first @boot line, before WiFi.

    The filesystem is not safe with both cores in it at once. A-NIX
    keeps core 1 off it: job and task output go through RAM, and
    loading commands and scripts (core 1) and writing logs and the
    history (core 0) take turns (a lock). A program's OWN file reads
    and writes on core 1 are not covered - load what it needs before
    it starts, and keep its messages in RAM.

    Your own program on core 1, from a .py command:
        import core1
        kb, why = core1.start(loop, (), "my program")
    core1.busy() says what runs there.

COMPILED PROGRAMS (.mpy)
    A program can be a compiled .mpy file instead of .py: typing its
    name, `run` and $PATH all find it (a .py of the same name is
    used first while both exist). Compile on a PC with mpy-cross of
    the SAME version as the board's MicroPython (see uname):
        pip install mpy-cross==1.29.0
        mpy-cross myprog.py          -> myprog.mpy
    .mpy files load faster and need much less RAM - the board skips
    compiling. /lib modules can be .mpy too (delete the .py). A .mpy
    can't be edited on the board, and its name must not be the same
    as a module (shell, net, os, ...): the shell refuses that.

THE PROMPT
    Up / Down          previous / next command (history)
    Left / Right       move in the line   Home / End (or ^A / ^E)
    Backspace / Del    delete             ^U / ^K  delete to start/end
    Tab                complete a command or file name; a second
                       Tab lists all matches
    ^L                 clear the screen
    Ctrl-C             while a command runs: stop it ($? = 130).
                       At the prompt: leave to the raw REPL (Ctrl-D
                       there restarts) - Thonny and mpremote rely
                       on this to connect.

    History is saved to HISTFILE after every command and loaded at
    shell start. `history` lists it numbered (`history 20`: the last
    20, `history -c`: clear it). Typed at the prompt:
        !!        run the last command again
        !N        run command number N (from `history`)
        !-N       the N-th last command
        !text     the last command starting with text
    The recalled command is shown before it runs. A line longer
    than the screen scrolls sideways.

COLOURS
    Like a Linux terminal (the default dircolors, grep --color and
    diff --color colours), only on the screen - never in a > file, a
    pipe, $(...), the background job's log or cron.log, so files stay
    plain text. `conf COLOR off` turns them all off, for a terminal
    that shows codes like [01;34m instead (Thonny's shell);
    `conf COLOR on` brings them back.

    banner      A-NIX bold cyan, the version bold yellow, the
                tagline magenta; the safe-mode notice yellow
    prompt      the folder bold blue; [N] job yellow, (low RAM) red
    ls, tree    folders bold blue, .py .mpy .sh bold green, archives
                and firmware (.zip .gz .tar .uf2 .bin) bold red,
                images (.png .jpg .gif .bmp) bold magenta
    grep        the match bold red, file name magenta, -n line
                number green
    diff        < removed lines red, > added green, 3c3 lines cyan
    error:      bold red, for every command
    dmesg       times green, a watchdog/unknown start and the crash
                details under it red, safe mode yellow
    selftest    ok green, WARN yellow, FAIL bold red
    cron, jobs, tasks, telnetd status, httpd status
                running and $?=0 green, failed and $?=1 red,
                stopped grey
    wifi status, scan   Connected green, addresses magenta, signal
                green (strong) / yellow / red (weak), open yellow
    pins        a pin reading 1 bold green
    hexdump     bytes coloured by kind (see HEXDUMP(1))
    man         headings bold
    edit        syntax colours like nano - see THE EDIT EDITOR

COMMANDS
    Builtins touch the shell's own process state (current directory,
    ENV) so, same as in a real sh, they can't be separate files -
    a child process can't change its parent's cwd or variables.
    Everything else lives in /bin and is found via $PATH.

CD(1)
    cd [path]           change directory (no path = $HOME)

PWD(1)
    pwd                 print the current directory

ECHO(1)
    echo [text]         print text
    echo [text] > file  overwrite file with text
    echo [text] >> file appends text to file

    Prints text exactly as typed - internal spacing (for column
    alignment, indentation) is preserved, unlike a command's normal
    arguments where repeated spaces collapse to one. Trade-off: * and
    ? in text print literally rather than matching files, since
    there's no tokenizing left to tell a pattern from plain text.

EXPORT(1)
    export              list all variables
    export KEY          show one variable
    export KEY=VAL      set a variable

UNSET(1)
    unset KEY           remove a variable

READ(1)
    read [VAR]          wait for Enter, or capture a line into VAR
                        (no prompt printed - echo your own first).
                        With < file or a pipe: VAR = its first line

LET(1)
    let VAR=expr        set VAR to an arithmetic result (as in ksh)

TIME(1)
    time cmd [args]     run cmd, then show how long it took
                        (time "a | b" for a pipe)

JOBS(1)
    jobs                the background job and its state
    jobs -f             follow its output live; any key stops following

KILL(1)
    kill                stop the background job

SPAWN(1)
    spawn file [args]   start an async program as a task - see TASK
                        SCHEDULER

TASKS(1)
    tasks               list tasks; tasks log [N]: what they printed;
                        tasks clean: forget finished ones

STOP(1)
    stop N | stop all   end a task / all tasks

SOURCE(1), .(1)
    source file.sh      run a script in this shell - its variables,
    . file.sh           functions, aliases and cd stay. (Every A-NIX
                        script runs that way - there are no
                        subshells - so this is the same as running it
                        by name, but takes any file name and never
                        searches $PATH.)

EXIT(1)
    exit                restart the shell (fresh ENV, output pins
                        driven low). Ctrl-C at the prompt leaves to
                        the raw REPL instead.

    /bin - one file per program, classic *NIX names used where
    this project has a real equivalent:

LS(1)
    ls [-l] [path...]  list directory contents, coloured like Linux
                        (see COLOURS); accepts * and ?
                        globs); several folders each get a heading,
                        -a is accepted (every entry is always
                        shown). Bare `ls` fills the terminal width
                        column by column (like real ls with no -l);
                        `-l` switches to one entry per line with a
                        d/- type flag and size, same idea as real
                        ls -l's mode+size columns without the actual
                        permission bits this filesystem doesn't have

CAT(1)
    cat file...         print files' contents (cat a > b copies)

CP(1)
    cp [-r] src... dst  copy file(s); -r for directories

MV(1)
    mv src... dst       move/rename file(s) into a directory

RM(1)
    rm [-r] file...     remove file(s); -r for directories

MKDIR(1)
    mkdir dir           create a directory

RMDIR(1)
    rmdir dir           remove an empty directory

PG(1)
    pg file             page a file, LINES lines at a time, Enter
                        to continue, q to stop (was more/less - SVR
                        pagers were pg, not more)

DIFF(1)
    diff file1 file2    show the lines that differ, like the real
                        diff: an inserted or deleted line shows up
                        as just that change. Normal diff format -
                        3c3 changed, 5a6,7 added, 8,9d7 deleted;
                        < old line, > new line. Prints nothing if
                        the files are identical. $? is 0 identical,
                        1 different, 2 trouble (missing file, or
                        too many changed lines to compare in RAM)

GREP(1)
    grep [-i] [-v] [-n] [-c] [-q] "text" file...   print lines that
                        contain text (or from a pipe: ls | grep .py).
                        -i any case, -v lines without it, -n line
                        numbers, -c only a count, -q nothing at all
                        (for if/&&). $? 1 if none

HEXDUMP(1)
    hexdump [-s START] [-n LEN] [-p] file   bytes in hex and as text,
                        16 per line (8 on a narrow screen), coloured
                        by kind: 00 grey, text normal, space/CR/LF
                        green, control magenta, 80-FF yellow; repeated
                        lines shown once as *. -p: no colour (also
                        none into a file or pipe, or with COLOR=off).
                        Also: cmd | hexdump

SHA256(1)
    sha256 file...      SHA-256 checksums, like sha256sum
    sha256 file SUM     check a file against a known checksum:
                        OK or FAILED ($? = 1). Also: cmd | sha256

SORT(1)
    sort [-r] [-n] [-u] [file]   sort lines: -r reverse, -n by
                        number, -u drop repeats. Holds all lines in
                        RAM

UNIQ(1)
    uniq [-c] [file]    drop repeated lines that follow each other
                        (-c: count them); sort | uniq drops all

CUT(1)
    cut -f LIST [-d SEP] [file]  fields split at SEP (default tab;
                        -d , for CSV, -d " " for spaces)
    cut -c LIST [file]  characters. LIST: 2  1,3  2-4  3-

TEE(1)
    ... | tee [-a] file pass the pipe on AND save a copy (-a
                        appends): ls | tee list.txt | wc

REPLACE(1)
    replace [-i] "old" "new" file  replace text in the file (written
                        safely), print how many; $? 1 if none
    ... | replace [-i] "old" "new" change piped text. -i any case;
                        "new" can be "" to delete

HEAD(1)
    head [-n N] file    first N lines (default 10); head -5 works too

TAIL(1)
    tail [-n N] file    last N lines (default 10); tail -5 works too

WC(1)
    wc file...          lines, words and bytes (plus a total)

TOUCH(1)
    touch file...       create empty files (existing ones untouched)

FIND(1)
    find [path] ["pattern"]   list everything below path (default
                        .) whose name matches pattern (* and ?);
                        quote the pattern so the shell doesn't
                        expand it. Directories end in /

DU(1)
    du [path]           size of every folder below path, and total
    du -s [path]        only the total       du -a: files too

STAT(1)
    stat path...        type, size and last change of files

WHICH(1)
    which name...       what a name runs: alias, builtin, or file

SEQ(1)
    seq [first [step]] last   numbers, one per line: seq 5,
                        seq 0 0.5 2, seq 10 1

TRUE(1)
    true                do nothing, $? 0       false: $? 1

FALSE(1)
    false               do nothing, $? 1 (for && / || and scripts)

LOG(1)
    log [-n] file [values]   append "date time,value,value" to a
                        CSV file; values from arguments or the first
                        line of a pipe. -n keeps only the numbers:
                        temp | log -n /root/temp.csv

PLOT(1)
    plot [-f N] [-d SEP] [-h ROWS] [-b] [file]   chart numbers in
                        the terminal: first number of each line, or
                        field N (CSV). -b bars. Many values are
                        averaged to fit. plot -f 2 /root/temp.csv

CLEAR(1)
    clear               clear the screen

UNAME(1)
    uname               show the shell version

DATE(1)
    date                      show the weekday, date and time
    date YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS  set the date and/or time; the weekday
                        is worked out automatically, impossible
                        dates are refused. The RTC has no battery -
                        it resets when power is lost.
    date ntp [server]         set from the internet (W boards,
                        default pool.ntp.org) - UTC plus $TZ hours

DF(1)
    df [-b] [path]      show total/used/free flash space, in KB
                        (-b = bytes)

MKFS(1)
    mkfs                erase everything inside $HOME (/root) -
                        A-NIX itself keeps working
    mkfs -a             erase the WHOLE filesystem, A-NIX included;
                        the board then starts to the raw REPL and
                        A-NIX must be copied back. Both ask first.

SLEEP(1)
    sleep seconds       wait (decimals ok; Ctrl-C stops it)
    sleep -l seconds    light sleep: far less current, then carry on
    sleep -d seconds    deep sleep: least current, then the board
                        restarts (@boot jobs run again). Both stop
                        the USB/telnet connection while asleep - for
                        battery projects, not the console. -l and -d
                        last at most 4294 s (71 min, the chip's timer)

WATCH(1)
    watch sec command...   clear the screen and run command every
                        sec seconds (0.1 to 86400) until q or Ctrl-C,
                        also over telnet: watch 1 in 14

DMESG(1)
    dmesg               show the boot log - see SAFE MODE AND THE
                        BOOT LOG, below
    dmesg -c            clear the log

CONF(1)
    conf                all settings in /etc/anix.conf, with values
    conf KEY            one setting
    conf KEY VALUE      change it - takes effect at once and is saved
    conf KEY none       unset it. Passwords show as ***.

CRON(1)
    cron                list scheduled jobs, next and last run
    cron log [N]        last N lines of the job log
    cron run N          run job N now, show its output
    cron reload         re-read /etc/crontab after editing it
    cron off | on       pause / resume - see SCHEDULED JOBS

SELFTEST(1)
    selftest [-q]       check the board and A-NIX: chip, firmware,
                        RAM, flash, file writing, clock, temperature
                        sensor, GPIO registers, WiFi/Bluetooth
                        modules, every /lib module and /bin command,
                        the /etc files, the manual and the license.
                        Switches no pins, connects nowhere. -q shows
                        only problems; $? = 1 if anything failed.
                        Run it first on every new board.

PS(1)
    ps                  what the board is doing: uptime, CPU, RAM,
                        flash, core 0 (shell, telnetd, httpd) / core 1
                        (job, tasks, your program),
                        WiFi, Bluetooth, watchdog, open buses, pins

REBOOT(1)
    reboot              full hardware reset, like pressing RESET
    reboot -s           soft reset, like Ctrl-D at the REPL - boot.py
                        and main.py run again, the RTC time and a
                        running watchdog carry on
                        Both are noted in the boot log (dmesg)
    restart             alias for reboot (set in /etc/profile)

LICENSE(1)
    license             show the MIT license text in the viewer
                        (q to leave)

EDIT(1)
    edit file           full-screen editor - see THE EDIT EDITOR,
                        below

ED(1)
    ed file             alias for `edit` (set in /etc/profile, so not
                        there in safe mode - use `edit`)

MAN(1)
    man [topic]         show this manual in the viewer: arrows,
                        PgUp/PgDn (or Space/b), Home/End, / search,
                        n next match, q to leave. With a topic, jumps
                        straight to that command's entry, or to a
                        section: man colours, man settings. Reads a
                        screen at a time - any size works

    /bin extras - no classic *NIX equivalent (this board has GPIO
    pins and a REPL), kept as ordinary
    /bin utilities alongside the above:

OUT(1)
    out pin|LED 0|1     drive a GPIO pin or the onboard LED. A new
                        output starts directly at the given level

IN(1)
    in pin|LED [up|down|none]   read a pin as input; also sets
                        $INn for scripts. down (default) = an
