HELP(1)
    help                quick one-line-per-command reference

    A bare name that isn't a builtin or a /bin command runs <name>
    or <name>.sh/.py from cwd, then from each $PATH directory - the
    normal way real shells resolve a typed command.

GPIO AND HARDWARE
    A pin's mode (in/out/analog/pwm) is set the first time you use
    it and remembered for the rest of the session - switch modes by
    just calling a different command on the same pin number; the
    pin is released from its old mode first (a running PWM is
    stopped), so `pins` always shows one mode per pin.

    GPIO 23, 24, 25 and 29 are refused by out/in/pwm/adc/httpd: on
    the W boards they drive the WiFi chip (driving them by hand
    kills WiFi and the LED), on the others they are board internals.
    Use LED for the onboard LED.

    UART and SPI buses work the same way: `uart <bus> init` / `spi
    <bus> init` configure bus 0 or 1 once, and every later `send`/
    `recv`/`xfer`/`write`/`read` on that bus number reuses it for
    the rest of the session - no need to re-init between calls.

NETWORKING
    WiFi, reachability, HTTP/HTTPS fetches, downloads and NTP time
    are handled by net.py (in /lib), behind wifi/scan/ping/curl/
    wget/date ntp - see COMMANDS, above, for each. Only the Pico W
    and Pico 2 W have WiFi; on the others these commands just say
    "no WiFi on this board".

    MicroPython exposes no raw ICMP socket, so `ping` can't send a
    real ICMP echo the way a PC's ping does - it substitutes a TCP
    connection-timing test, which answers the same practical
    question without being literally the same measurement.

THE WEB SERVER (httpd)
    `httpd` starts a minimal HTTP server (server.py, in /lib) on core
    0, like telnetd: the prompt watches its socket and answers a
    browser at once while it waits for keys. Core 1 stays free for
    your own programs. While a command runs, a browser waits until it
    ends; a big download or upload holds the prompt for its few
    seconds (keys typed meanwhile aren't lost). The pin panel needs no
    JavaScript - every request re-renders the current state of
    whatever pins you gave it:

        pin number, or LED    ON/OFF buttons
        PWMn                  duty-cycle field (0-100) + Set button
        INn                   read-only digital value
        ADCn                  read-only raw value + voltage (0-2)

    With no pins given, httpd uses WEB_PINS from /etc/anix.conf;
    WEB_PINS=none means no pins at all - http://IP/ then opens the
    file browser directly and the LED stays free for your programs.

    The panel uses the shell's own pins - the same ones out/in/pwm/
    adc/pins see - so the shell and the browser always agree.
    Outputs already set keep their level, new ones start low.

    A password is required (-p, or HTTPD_PASS). The browser asks for
    a login: any user name, that password. It's plain HTTP, so this
    keeps out people who don't know the password, not someone
    capturing traffic on the same network.

    If any inputs or ADCs are configured, the page auto-refreshes
    every 2s. `httpd stop` closes it at once.

    httpd prints the address to open (or, without WiFi yet, that the
    panel is reachable once `wifi` connects). After that the server
    stays quiet - it never writes into your prompt; problems (a
    request that failed, a server that could not start) show in
    `httpd status` as "last: ...".

    The file browser (Files link on the panel, or http://IP/files)
    shows the whole filesystem from /. Click a folder to open it, a
    file to download it, view to see it as text. upload here sends
    one or more files from your PC into the folder shown (written to
    a .tmp file and renamed when complete - a broken upload changes
    nothing); new folder, rename and delete do what they say (delete
    asks first; a folder must be empty). Full access means full:
    deleting /lib, /bin, boot.py or main.py breaks A-NIX exactly as
    rm would. Speed is roughly 50-150 KB/s; one request at a time.
    conf HTTPD_FILES off leaves the browser out of the panel.
    Anything that changes something (pins, upload, delete...) is only
    accepted from the panel's own pages: a link on another web site,
    or a URL typed into the address bar, gets 403 - so a strange web
    page can't use your saved login to switch pins or delete files.

THE TELNET SERVER (telnetd)
    `telnetd` puts the A-NIX console on the network, for PuTTY
    (Connection type: Other -> Telnet, NOT SSH; port 23) or any
    telnet client. It is the SAME console as USB, not a second one:
    both see everything, and keys from either reach the shell.

    A password is required: -p, else TELNET_PASS in /etc/anix.conf
    (conf TELNET_PASS <password>). A new client gets the banner and
    "password:"; nothing of the console reaches it before the right
    password, and three wrong tries close the connection. After it,
    the prompt appears at once.

    One client at a time. A client that hasn't logged in within 30 s,
    or has sent nothing for 10 minutes, is replaced by the next one
    that connects. Closing the PuTTY window ends the session; you can
    connect again at once. New clients are taken while the shell
    waits at its prompt (about once a second), not while a command
    runs. Ctrl-C acts as on USB, but reaches a running command only
    if that command reads keys (watch, edit, nc ...).

    `telnetd status` shows the port, the client and the last problem
    (e.g. a client that left before logging in). Not encrypted - fine
    at home, not on a public network. Gone after a reset: put
    `@boot wifi -r 5` and `@boot telnetd` in /etc/crontab to have it
    after every boot. telnetd and httpd both run on core 0, at the
    same time, and leave core 1 free.

SCHEDULED JOBS (cron)
    /etc/crontab lists commands to run by themselves, one per line:
        @boot <command>          once when the board starts (right
                                 after /etc/startup, before the prompt)
        every <N>s|m|h|d <cmd>   repeatedly, from N seconds up to 6
                                 days (every 30m, every 6h)
        at HH:MM <command>       every day at that time - needs the
                                 clock set (date ntp)
    A # starts a comment, but only at the start of a line.

    Jobs run on core 0 while the shell waits at its prompt with
    nothing typed, never in the middle of another command - so an
    `every` or `at` job that falls due while a command runs waits
    until it ends (an `at` job whose minute passes meanwhile is
    skipped that day). Their output goes to /var/log/cron.log, never
    to the screen: see it with `cron log` - by default only for jobs
    that failed ($? not 0), so a job every minute doesn't write the
    flash every minute; `conf CRON_LOG all` logs every run, off
    nothing. `cron` shows every job's last run and $? anyway. Commands
    that need keys (edit, read, choice ...) are refused. After editing
    the file, `cron reload` (or reboot). Safe mode (BOOTSEL) skips the
    file.

BLUETOOTH
    `ble scan` and `ble advertise` (W boards only) use the board's
    built-in BLE radio directly - no GATT client/server here, just
    discovery and simple peripheral advertising. The radio, once
    turned on by either command, stays active for the rest of the
    session. `ble advertise` with no seconds given keeps advertising
    until you run `ble advertise stop`.

WATCHDOG
    `wdt start [timeout_ms]` arms the hardware watchdog - this is a
    one-way door on every Pico: MicroPython has no way to disarm a
    running watchdog, only feed it. Once started, `wdt feed` (or a
    script feeding it on a timer) has to keep happening inside the
    timeout or the board resets. There's no `wdt stop`.

    The shell watchdog does the feeding for you: `conf WATCHDOG on`
    (it starts at the next shell start). A timer feeds the watchdog
    every second, but only while MicroPython is alive - if the board
    locks up (a driver stuck, the VM frozen) it resets within 8 s
    and the boot log says "watchdog". With `conf WATCHDOG_HANG N` it
    also resets when the shell has shown no sign of life for N
    minutes: no prompt, no command starting, no output. A command
    that runs silently that long counts as stuck - and so does an
    editor or `read` left waiting - so use it on unattended boards.
    sleep, watch and `in -w` tell the shell they're waiting on purpose
    and don't count as stuck.
    Turning it off: `conf WATCHDOG off`, then `reboot`. boot.py starts
    the watchdog and feeds it at every start - also after a soft reset
    from Thonny, mpremote or the PC menu - so those keep working with
    WATCHDOG=on (at the bare REPL the board resets only if it really
    freezes). Safe mode (BOOTSEL) skips it. `sleep -l` / `-d` are
    refused while it runs.

SETTINGS
    /etc/anix.conf holds the settings, one KEY=VALUE per line, with a
    comment above each explaining it. `conf` lists and changes them
    (no editing needed); "none" means not set. Besides WiFi, TZ,
    passwords, httpd, I2C and flash pins (see ENVIRONMENT):
    PROFILE, STARTUP, CRONTAB, HISTORY
                on (default) / off: whether the shell reads
                /etc/profile, runs /etc/startup and loads
                /etc/crontab at start. off = not read at all, so it
                takes no RAM and leaves no holes in it. PROFILE=off
                means no aliases and no saved history, PATH=/bin,
                HOME=/root. With CRONTAB=off no jobs run and `cron`
                says so. HISTORY=off: no prompt history (Up/Down,
                !!, `history`) in RAM, none saved. Takes effect at
                the next start (`reboot`).
    CRON_LOG    errors (default) = cron logs only jobs that failed,
                all = every run, off = nothing (see SCHEDULED JOBS)
    LOG_MAX     bytes a log in /var/log may reach before it keeps
                only its newest half (default 16384). Applies to
                boot.log, cron.log and tasks.log; the background
                job's log stops at 4x this.
    MEM_LOW     free RAM in bytes (default 24576) below which the
                shell, after a command, unloads big modules nothing is
                using - editor, script, syntax, tui, view, hexfmt,
                biggest first, only until a third more is free (each
                unload and reload leaves holes in RAM); they load
                again when needed. The prompt shows (low RAM) until
                then. The small, often used ones stay unless RAM runs
                out completely. Not while a background job or tasks
                run.
    EDIT_MEM    biggest file `edit` opens, in % of free RAM (default
                40, 10-90). The editor needs about twice the file size
                plus room for undo and copy/paste; if it runs out of
                memory anyway it keeps the text and asks you to save.
    WATCHDOG, WATCHDOG_HANG   see WATCHDOG
    COLOR       on (default) / off: Linux-style colours on the
                screen - see COLOURS.
    An older /etc/profile with settings in it still works (profile is read
    last, so its values win).

    RAM at start: /etc/anix.conf, /etc/profile, /etc/crontab and the
    history are read one line at a time and comments are skipped, so
    comments cost no RAM; scripts keep a comment as an empty line. The
    shell runs gc.collect() before each part that stays in RAM, so it
    packs together and leaves the largest free block big (see FREE(1)).

SAFE MODE AND THE BOOT LOG
    Every boot adds a line to /var/log/boot.log: its number, the RTC
    time, why the board started (power on, watchdog, reboot, reboot
    -s, soft reset) and the mode (shell, or safe mode). `dmesg` shows
    the log; it is kept under 16 KB (the newest half stays).

    Safe mode: hold BOOTSEL while the board starts - press it right
    after power is applied (held BEFORE power-on, it starts the USB
    bootloader instead). The shell then starts without /etc/profile
    (no aliases; PATH, HOME and history use their defaults), without
    /etc/startup and /etc/crontab (no @boot jobs, nothing starts by
    itself) and without the shell watchdog - so a broken @boot line,
    startup script or watchdog setting can't lock the board. The
    banner and the boot log say so. Reboot without BOOTSEL to leave.

    If the shell itself can't be loaded or crashes (a broken file
    after an upload, out of memory), main.py writes the error to the
    boot log and stops at the bare MicroPython REPL with a message.

    The RTC has no battery, so after a power cut the logged times
    restart from the firmware's default date.

THE WHIPTAIL DIALOGS
    Boxes are drawn as an overlay wherever the cursor already is,
    same as real whiptail - not a full-screen takeover, so whatever
    was on screen stays visible around the box. Plain ASCII borders
    (+/-/|), not Unicode box-drawing, for the same reason `tree`
    avoids box-drawing glyphs: not every terminal on the other end
    of this board's connection can be assumed to render them.

    menu   Up/Down move (wraps at the ends), PgUp/PgDn jump a
           screenful, Enter selects, Esc or q cancels
    msgbox Enter, Esc, space, or q all dismiss it - there's only
           one outcome
    yesno  Left/Right or Tab toggles the highlighted button, Enter
           confirms it, y/n answer directly, Esc = No
    inputbox   type to insert, Left/Right/Home/End/Backspace/Delete
           to edit, Enter confirms, Esc cancels

    A bare Escape keypress and the start of an arrow-key sequence
    both begin with the same byte on the wire - Esc is only counted
    as Cancel if no second byte follows within 50ms, since a real
    escape sequence's bytes arrive together. Screen size is asked of
    the terminal the same way `edit` does, for the same reason: to
    center the box correctly instead of guessing.

WRITING SHELL SCRIPTS
    A script is a .sh file, run by typing its name (or `run x.sh`,
    or `. x.sh`), with arguments: backup.sh /root/data -> $1. It
    is checked as a whole first: a missing fi or
    done is reported with its line number before anything runs.

    if COND [then]          if/elif/else/fi, for/while/done can be
        ...                 nested in any way
    elif COND [then]
        ...
    else
        ...
    fi
    for VAR START END [STEP] [do]     $VAR counts START..END
        ...                           (STEP default 1, or -1 when
    done                              counting down; 0 refused)
    while COND [do]                   repeats while COND is true
        ...
    done
    break / continue    leave the loop / go to its next round
    :label / goto label jump to a top-level label - also out of
                        loops; not into a block
    function NAME       define a function - then call NAME like a
        ...             command, with arguments ($1.. inside); it
    end                 is known until the shell restarts
    return [N]          leave a function (or script) with $? = N
    exit                end the script (not the shell)
    set -x / set +x     show each command before it runs, on/off.
                        Off by default; a leading @ silences one line
    # text              comment

    COND can be:
        exist PATH          file or folder exists
        A == B   A != B     equal / not equal
        A < B  A > B  A <= B  A >= B
                            compared as numbers when both are
                            numbers, else as text
        COMMAND [args]      run it - true if it succeeded ($? 0):
                            if grep -q error log.txt then
        not COND   C1 and C2   C1 or C2
    Variables are expanded each time a condition is checked, so
    `while $N < 10` sees N change:
        let N=0
        while $N < 10 do
            let N=N+1
        done

THE EDIT EDITOR
    A small nano-style editor. The title bar shows the cursor
    position (L12:C5), the file name and whether it's modified.
    Only changed screen rows are redrawn, in one write, so it
    doesn't flicker over a serial link.

    Screen size is asked of the terminal itself (the standard cursor-
    position query) rather than trusted from COLUMNS/LINES alone; a
    terminal that doesn't answer within ~200ms falls back to
    COLUMNS/LINES (default 80x24).

    The whole file is held in memory as separate lines, so a file
    bigger than ~40% of free RAM (`free`) is refused rather than
    attempted. `pg` streams a page at a time and has no such limit.

        arrows Home End PgUp PgDn   move (long lines scroll sideways)
        Ctrl+F     go to line         Ctrl+W     find; Ctrl+W then
                                                 Enter finds next
        Ctrl+B     mark on/off - then move to select
        Ctrl+A     select all         Ctrl+K     cut (selection, else
                                                 the whole line)
        Ctrl+Y     copy (same rule)   Ctrl+V     paste
        Ctrl+Z     undo               Tab        indent
        Ctrl+O     save               Ctrl+X     exit (asks to save)
        Ctrl+H     help screen (also Ctrl+G)

    Typing or pasting over a selection replaces it. Enter keeps the
    line's indentation and adds one level after a line ending in
    ":". Text pasted from the terminal keeps its own indentation and
    is drawn once at the end, not character by character.

    Undo goes back over the last 20 changes, using at most 8 KB of
    RAM (UNDO_MAX_STEPS / UNDO_MAX_KB at the top of /lib/editor.py);
    when full, the oldest change is dropped. A run of typing on one
    line counts as one change.

    Saving writes file.tmp first and renames it over the original,
    so a power cut mid-save leaves the old file intact.

    Syntax colours, like nano's (COLOR=off turns them off; files
    of other kinds open without colours):
      .py      keywords bold cyan, def/class names bold blue, calls
               yellow, assigned names bold white, strings green,
               numbers red, True/False/None/self magenta, comments
               cyan, @decorators bold magenta
      .sh and /etc/startup   if/for/while/... bold cyan, the command
               word bold blue, $VARIABLES green, "strings" yellow,
               :labels bold yellow, | ; && > operators magenta
      /etc/crontab   @boot/every/at bold yellow, the time magenta,
               the command like a script line
      .conf .cfg .ini, /etc/profile   KEY= bold cyan, on/off/none
               magenta, numbers red, comments cyan
    The rows on screen are kept coloured in a small cache, so typing
    re-colours only the line you are on (/lib/syntax.py). If RAM runs
    short, rows are simply shown without colours.

    Ctrl+C is never used - it's MicroPython's interrupt key. Ctrl+H
    is the byte some terminals send for Backspace: PuTTY's default
    Backspace ("Control-?") is right; if Backspace opens the help
    screen, set it back. Ctrl+E also exits, for mpremote, whose own
    terminal takes Ctrl+X. A key the editor doesn't recognise shows
    its code on the message line.

